/*  A read-eval-print loop for Cufet, written in Cufet.

    Slice 1: every line you type is a whole program. It is written to a scratch file and handed to
    `cufet`, and whatever that prints comes back here. Nothing is remembered between lines yet —
    `Define x as 5.` on one line and `State x.` on the next are two programs, and the second has
    never heard of the first. Keeping the bindings is the next slice.

    Why it shells out rather than evaluating here: `cufet` already holds the type checker, and its
    refusals are the point of the language. An evaluator written in this file would have to say
    something worse about a bad line than the compiler already says about it.

    `Quit!!!` on a line by itself exits; so does end of input.

    Run it interpreted. Compiling it needs POSIX, because `run` is fork + execvp — the same
    constraint the shell carries.  */

/* Somewhere to put the line before handing it over. TMPDIR is POSIX, TEMP is Windows, and /tmp is
   the answer that is still right when a program is started with neither set. */
Bind text to scratch-file:
    Define folder as the environment variable "TMPDIR" but void is "".
    If the length of folder is 0, folder becomes the environment variable "TEMP" but void is "".
    If the length of folder is 0, folder becomes "/tmp".
    Return "{folder}/cufet-repl-line.cufe".
Done.

/* Errors before output, because a refusal is what explains an empty result. Both arrive already
   captured, so this is the only place either is shown. */
Bind void to show, given (the text printed, the text complained):
    If the length of complained is greater than 0, state complained trimmed.
    If the length of printed is greater than 0, state printed trimmed.
Done.

/* Writing the file and launching can each fail — a full disk, or no `cufet` on the PATH. Both are
   failure values, so a bad line costs a message and not the session. */
Bind void to evaluate, given (the text line, the text scratch):
    Try to:
        Write line to the file scratch.
        Define answered as run "cufet" with arguments (scratch).
        Cast show on (the output of answered, the errors of answered).
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        State "cufet-repl: {the message of the failure}".
    Done.
Done.

State "Cufet. Type Quit!!! to leave, or close the input".

Define scratch as cast scratch-file.
Define running as true.
While running, repeat:
    Define raw as read a line from the input.

    /* Void is end of input — the stream closed instead of anyone saying Quit!!! */
    If raw is void, running becomes false.
    If raw is not void:
        Define line as raw trimmed.
        If line is "Quit!!!", running becomes false.
        /* A blank line is not an error, it is just nothing to do. */
        If line is not "Quit!!!" and the length of line is greater than 0:
            Cast evaluate on (line, scratch).
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
